out.push(note('Teacher\'s note. Four faults to watch, and the remedy for each. First, the figure without a basis, which is not a writing fault but a knowledge fault: a learner who cannot name the base of a percentage has not been careless, they have inherited a number. The remedy is the question "where did this come from?", asked about the figures in their own standing documents, and it should be asked out loud in the room because the answers are often genuinely unknown. Expect at least one learner to discover that a figure in regular use was calculated on a base nobody now remembers, and make that the example for the rest of the unit. Second, the trend drawn from two or three points. It is the commonest analytical fault in business English and it hides inside respectable sentences — "the rate of growth is slowing" needs four points and almost always has two. Drill the count, not the judgement: how many points, and what does the fourth one cost to get? Third, the frequency stripped out as a one-off. The diagnostic is the denominator. "One Monday in three years" is an event; "twice in thirty hires" is a rate, and they are the same facts. Teach learners to ask how many opportunities there were, and the classification becomes arithmetic instead of temperament. Watch especially for the undeclared variety in 8.4\'s free writing — an adjustment nobody wrote down cannot be withdrawn, because there is no document to withdraw it from, and in the series\' own trial every learner found one of these somewhere in their own reporting. Fourth, and this is the one that outlives the unit: the bridge not built. Learners accept the principle immediately and do not act on it, because building a bridge means obtaining the reader\'s own record, which takes a telephone call to somebody outside their department. Set it as the 8.6 homework and collect it specifically. One practical note on 8.7. The four-part telephone answer — figure, basis, comparison against yourself, cause with two earlier periods — is drillable in five minutes and should be drilled in pairs until nobody prefaces. The fault to listen for is not hesitation but the word "well", which is the sound of a prepared reason arriving before the number it is meant to explain. And the thing worth saying to a class at the end of Unit 8 is Mr Haddad\'s: he was not finding out what the debtor days were, because he could work that out himself. Everything in seven units about what goes in a document exists so that the four seconds on the telephone can be used properly.'));
